          "session_id": "full-mt-m-007",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I was told my thyroid is underactive.",
          "answer": "**What it means:** Hypothyroidism means the thyroid gland makes too little thyroid hormone. That hormone sets how fast many body tissues work, so a shortfall can slow things down. The pituitary gland, in the brain, responds to low levels by releasing more thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH). That is why TSH is usually the first blood test. (Source: *Hypothyroidism (underactive thyroid)*, general clinical reference)\n\n**How it is classified:**\n- **Primary** hypothyroidism, where the thyroid gland itself is at fault, is by far the most common. **Central** hypothyroidism, where the pituitary or hypothalamus is at fault, is uncommon. (Same source)\n- **Overt** hypothyroidism means raised TSH with low free thyroxine. **Subclinical** hypothyroidism means raised TSH with free thyroxine still in the normal range. (*Hypothyroidism: which test comes first*, endocrine guidance)\n- In the ICD-10-CM coding system, thyroid disorders sit in the block E00 to E07, within the endocrine chapter. The passages I retrieved did not give the exact code for hypothyroidism, so please check that with your clinician or records. (*ICD-10-CM Chapter 4*)\n\n**Causes:** The retrieved passages say that thyroid antibody tests can show autoimmune thyroiditis, an immune-driven cause, and that this test helps judge how likely mild disease is to progress. They did not give a full list of causes, so I can't cover them further here.\n\n**Next steps and lifestyle:** I could not get lifestyle guidance for this condition. The lifestyle tool returned an error, so I have no sourced advice on diet, activity, or sleep. Treatment decisions, including whether any medication is needed, are for your clinician to make with you.\n\n**What you might ask your clinician:** What type of underactive thyroid I have (overt or subclinical), what caused it, when my thyroid levels will be rechecked, and what symptoms should prompt me to seek care sooner.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
